WEBVTT

00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:03.520
Modern post-quantum planning should begin with vetted standards and mature

00:00:03.520 --> 00:00:04.600
implementations,

00:00:04.600 --> 00:00:05.476
not custom cryptography.

00:00:05.476 --> 00:00:09.465
NIST's post-quantum standardization work gives organizations a practical reference point

00:00:09.465 --> 00:00:12.500
for algorithms intended to address major public-key use cases.

00:00:12.500 --> 00:00:16.095
This course is not official NIST training, and standards will continue to evolve,

00:00:16.095 --> 00:00:18.000
but the main engineering message is stable:

00:00:18.000 --> 00:00:21.920
use reviewed algorithms through well-maintained libraries and protocols rather than

00:00:21.920 --> 00:00:23.000
inventing new designs.

00:00:23.000 --> 00:00:28.000
ML-KEM is the major key-encapsulation concept teams will see in PQC migration discussions.

00:00:28.000 --> 00:00:28.720
At a high level,

00:00:28.720 --> 00:00:33.000
it supports establishing shared secret material in a way intended to resist both classical

00:00:33.000 --> 00:00:36.000
and cryptographically relevant quantum attacks.

00:00:36.000 --> 00:00:39.000
It is not a drop-in answer for every protocol by itself.

00:00:39.000 --> 00:00:42.965
It still has to be integrated into TLS, VPNs, APIs, products,

00:00:42.965 --> 00:00:47.220
certificates where applicable, and operational workflows that expect certain sizes,

00:00:47.220 --> 00:00:49.000
timings, and negotiation behavior.

00:00:49.000 --> 00:00:54.470
ML-DSA and SLH-DSA are digital signature concepts used for different post-quantum

00:00:54.470 --> 00:00:56.000
signature needs.

00:00:56.000 --> 00:00:59.980
ML-DSA is a lattice-based signature family, while SLH-DSA is stateless and hash-based.

00:00:59.980 --> 00:01:03.960
Practitioners do not need heavy math to understand the planning impact.

00:01:03.960 --> 00:01:07.000
They need to know which systems need signatures, who verifies them,

00:01:07.000 --> 00:01:10.005
how long signatures must remain valid, how large artifacts become,

00:01:10.005 --> 00:01:13.000
and whether the surrounding infrastructure can handle the new formats.

00:01:13.000 --> 00:01:16.965
Algorithms also come with parameter sets and security categories.

00:01:16.965 --> 00:01:20.090
Those choices are not decorative; they influence security level, performance,

00:01:20.090 --> 00:01:21.838
artifact size, and interoperability.

00:01:21.838 --> 00:01:25.775
A standards-aware migration still requires testing, monitoring, governance,

00:01:25.775 --> 00:01:27.000
and rollback planning.

00:01:27.000 --> 00:01:30.190
Mature adoption means following reputable implementation guidance,

00:01:30.190 --> 00:01:33.005
tracking library and vendor support, documenting decisions,

00:01:33.005 --> 00:01:36.000
and avoiding unsupported combinations that only work in a lab.
